{"cuisine/sabahan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sabahan-cuisine","n":"Sabahan cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Malaysia","intro":[["Sabahan cuisine is a regional cuisine of Malaysia. As in the rest of Malaysian cuisine, Sabah food is based on staples such as rice with a great variety of other ingredients and different methods of food preparations due to the influence of the state's varied geography and indigenous cultures that were quite distinct from the regional cuisines of the Peninsular Malaysia. Sabah along with its neighbour of Sarawak is famous for their multi-ethnic population."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"sabahan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabahan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Tamu: A Guest at the Bornean Table","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Bryan Koh","note":"Recipes shared by families across Borneo, not this region alone.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Malaysian cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/44/Linopot_om_sambal_tuhau.jpg/500px-Linopot_om_sambal_tuhau.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Rombituon","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linopot_om_sambal_tuhau.jpg"}},"cuisine/sundanese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sundanese-cuisine","n":"Sundanese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Sundanese cuisine is the cuisine of the Sundanese people of Western Java and Banten, Indonesia. Sundanese food is characterised by its freshness; the well-known lalab eaten with sambal and also karedok demonstrate the Sundanese fondness for fresh raw vegetables. Unlike the rich and spicy taste, infused with coconut milk and curry, of Minangkabau cuisine, Sundanese cuisine has a simple and clear taste, ranging from savoury salty to fresh sourness to mild sweetness to hot and spicy."],["Sambal terasi is the most important and the most common condiment in Sundanese cuisine, and is eaten together with lalab or fried tofu and tempeh. Sayur asem vegetable tamarind soup is a common vegetable soup in Sundanese cuisine. Common soups containing meat are soto Bandung, a soup of beef and daikon radish, and mie kocok noodle soup with beef meat and kikil."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"sundanese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sundanese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f9/Sundanese_Food_01.JPG/500px-Sundanese_Food_01.JPG","w":500,"h":384,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sundanese_Food_01.JPG"}},"cuisine/palestinian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/palestinian-cuisine","n":"Palestinian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Palestine","intro":[["Palestinian cuisine consists of foods from or commonly eaten by Palestinians or in the region of Palestine, whether in Palestine, Israel, Jordan, or refugee camps in nearby countries, or by the Palestinian diaspora. The cuisine is a diffusion of the cultures of civilizations that settled in the region of Palestine, particularly during and after the Islamic era beginning with the Arab Umayyad conquest, then the eventual Persian-influenced Abbasids and ending with the strong influences of Turkish cuisine, resulting from the coming of the Ottoman Turks. It is similar to other Levantine cuisines, including Lebanese, Syrian and Jordanian."],["Rice and variations of kibbee are common in the Galilee. West Bank  cuisine consists  of taboon bread, rice and meat, while  fish  and lentils are staples on the coast. Gaza cuisine is more diverse in terms of seafood and spices, with widespread use of chili peppers."],["The area is also home to many desserts, ranging from those made regularly and those that are commonly reserved for the holidays. Most Palestinian sweets are pastries filled with either sweetened cheeses, dates or various nuts such as almonds, walnuts or pistachios. Beverages could also depend on holidays such as during Ramadan, when carob, tamarind and apricot juices are consumed at sunset. Coffee is consumed throughout the day and liquor is not very prevalent among the population; however, some alcoholic beverages such as arak or beer are consumed by Christians and some Muslims."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"palestinian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palestinian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Zaitoun: Recipes and Stories from the Palestinian Kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Yasmin Khan, 2018","note":"Recipes collected while traveling through the West Bank, Gaza and Israel.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781408883846","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781408883846"},{"name":"Falastin: A Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sami Tamimi and Tara Wigley, 2020","note":"Palestinian recipes from the Jerusalem-born Ottolenghi co-founder.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":13,"links":[["Jibneh Arabieh","dish/jibneh-arabieh"],["Kibbeh","dish/kibbeh"],["Maltout","dish/maltout"],["Maqluba","dish/maqluba"],["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Musakhan","dish/musakhan"],["Ntolmás","dish/ntolmas"],["Qalayet bandora","dish/qalayet-bandora"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"],["Stuffed squash","dish/stuffed-squash"],["Stuffed turnips","dish/stuffed-turnips"],["Taboon bread","dish/taboon-bread"],["Zalabiyeh","dish/zalabiyeh"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Pine nut","ingredient/pine-nut"],["Pistachio nut","ingredient/pistachio-nut"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Goat milk","ingredient/goat-milk"],["Sheep milk","ingredient/sheep-milk"],["Bulgur","ingredient/bulgur"],["Levantine cuisine","ingredient/levantine-cuisine"],["Extra virgin olive oil","ingredient/extra-virgin-olive-oil"],["Anise","ingredient/anise"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Pistachio","ingredient/pistachio"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Allspice","ingredient/allspice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/06/Palestinianbreakfastfalafel.jpg/500px-Palestinianbreakfastfalafel.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Peteravivangel","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palestinianbreakfastfalafel.jpg"}},"cuisine/wallis-and-futuna-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/wallis-and-futuna-cuisine","n":"Wallis and Futuna cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Wallis and Futuna Is.","intro":[["Wallis and Futuna, an overseas territory of France in Oceania, has a rich Polynesian culture that is very similar to the cultures of its neighbouring nations Samoa and Tonga. The Wallisian and Futunan cultures share very similar components in language, dance, cuisine and modes of celebration."],["Fishing and agriculture are the traditional practices and most people live in traditional fate houses in an oval shape made of thatch. Kava, as with many Polynesian islands, is a popular beverage brewed in the two islands, and is a traditional offering in rituals. Highly detailed tapa cloth art is a specialty of Wallis and Futuna."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"wallis-and-futuna-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallis_and_Futuna_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Wallis and Futuna Tourism: Island savours","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"wallis-futuna.travel","note":"Official tourism overview of local food such as umu earth-oven cooking, raw fish in coconut milk and faikai; no full recipes.","url":"https://www.wallis-futuna.travel/en/discover-our-islands/island-savours","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine"},"dish/bir-pletok":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bir-pletok","n":"Bir pletok","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Bir pletok is a non-alcoholic Indonesian drink of the Betawi people in Jakarta, Indonesia. Pletok beer is made from several spices, namely ginger, pandan leaves, boiled sappan wood, and lemongrass."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bir-pletok"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bir_pletok"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Betawi cuisine","cuisine/betawi-cuisine"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":10,"links":[["Biancaea sappan","ingredient/biancaea-sappan"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Illicium verum","ingredient/illicium-verum"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Nigella sativa","ingredient/nigella-sativa"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Piper retrofractum","ingredient/piper-retrofractum"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cd/Bir_Pletok.JPG/500px-Bir_Pletok.JPG","w":500,"h":332,"by":"Ezagren (bicara / talk)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bir_Pletok.JPG"}},"ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate","n":"Sodium bicarbonate","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Sodium bicarbonate, commonly known as baking soda or bicarbonate of soda, or saleratus, is a chemical compound with the chemical formula NaHCO3. It is a salt composed of a sodium cation (Na+) and a bicarbonate anion (HCO3−)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sodium-bicarbonate"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_bicarbonate"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"]]],["Gujarati cuisine","cuisine/gujarati-cuisine",[["Dhokla","dish/dhokla"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Malic acid","ingredient/malic-acid"],["Tartaric acid","ingredient/tartaric-acid"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lentil","ingredient/lentil"],["Mustard oil","ingredient/mustard-oil"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Leavening agent","ingredient/leavening-agent"],["Malt extract","ingredient/malt-extract"],["Wholemeal","ingredient/wholemeal"],["Apples","ingredient/apples"]]}]},"ingredient/palm-fruit-juice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/palm-fruit-juice","n":"Palm fruit juice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Palm fruit juice or Tal er Rosh, is sweet sap extracted from the fruit of palm trees in summer. It contains vitamins A, B, C, and the elements zinc, calcium, potassium, and iron. It also contains antioxidants. This is a symbolic and well-known food in Bengal in the summer. Numbers of popular Bengali foods are made using this palm fruit juice such as palm candy, pitha, pays, kheer, and taller boora (Bengali: তালের বড়া)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-palm-fruit-juice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palm_fruit_juice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",[["Edo black soup","dish/edo-black-soup"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Palm oil","ingredient/palm-oil"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/53/Juice_seller_at_chawkbazar_iftar_market_15.jpg/500px-Juice_seller_at_chawkbazar_iftar_market_15.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Wasiul Bahar","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juice_seller_at_chawkbazar_iftar_market_15.jpg"}},"dish/flummery":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/flummery","n":"Flummery","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Flummery is a starch-based, sweet, soft dessert pudding which originated in Great Britain during the early modern period. The word has also been used for other semi-set desserts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-flummery"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flummery"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["White wine","ingredient/white-wine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Battenberg cake","dish/battenberg-cake"],["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Bread sauce","dish/bread-sauce"],["Carpetbag steak","dish/carpetbag-steak"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Crumble","dish/crumble"],["Crumpet","dish/crumpet"],["Curry in the United Kingdom","dish/curry-in-the-united-kingdom"],["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Dundee cake","dish/dundee-cake"],["English muffin","dish/english-muffin"],["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"],["Garibaldi biscuit","dish/garibaldi-biscuit"],["Hot cross bun","dish/hot-cross-bun"],["Hovis biscuit","dish/hovis-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"],["Melba toast","dish/melba-toast"],["Mince pie","dish/mince-pie"],["Phall","dish/phall"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Ploughman's lunch","dish/ploughman-s-lunch"],["Potted shrimps","dish/potted-shrimps"],["Prawn cocktail, steak and Black Forest gateau","dish/prawn-cocktail-steak-and-black-forest-gateau"],["Rich tea","dish/rich-tea"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"],["Scotch egg","dish/scotch-egg"],["Shepherd's pie","dish/shepherd-s-pie"],["Soldiers (food)","dish/soldiers-food"],["Spotted dick","dish/spotted-dick"],["Tea sandwich","dish/tea-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/46/Flum1.JPG/500px-Flum1.JPG","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Jrej09","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flum1.JPG"}},"ingredient/chilli-pepper":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/chilli-pepper","n":"Chilli pepper","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Chili peppers, also spelled chile or chilli, are varieties of berry-fruit plants from the genus Capsicum, which are members of the nightshade family Solanaceae, cultivated for their pungency (spicy heat). They are used as a spice to add pungency in many cuisines. Capsaicin and the related capsaicinoids give chili peppers their intensity when ingested or applied topically. Chili peppers exhibit a range of heat and flavors. This diversity is the reason behind the availability of different types of chili powder, each offering its own taste and heat level."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-chilli-pepper"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chilli_pepper"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Gulai","dish/gulai"],["Sambal","dish/sambal"],["Sambal tuktuk","dish/sambal-tuktuk"]]],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food",[["Gulai","dish/gulai"]]],["Singaporean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired",[["Gulai","dish/gulai"]]],["Singaporean cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine",[["Gulai","dish/gulai"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Anchovies as food","ingredient/anchovies-as-food"],["Shrimp paste","ingredient/shrimp-paste"],["Bird","ingredient/bird"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Bumbu (seasoning)","ingredient/bumbu-seasoning"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Fennel","ingredient/fennel"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/50/Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg/500px-Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg","w":500,"h":400,"by":"Takeaway","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg"}},"ingredient/ostreidae":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ostreidae","n":"Ostreidae","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The Ostreidae, the true oysters, include most species of molluscs commonly consumed as oysters. Pearl oysters are not true oysters, and belong to the order Pteriida."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ostreidae"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostreidae"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Gumbo","dish/gumbo"]]],["Cajun cuisine","cuisine/cajun-cuisine",[["Gumbo","dish/gumbo"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Brachyura","ingredient/brachyura"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Filé powder","ingredient/file-powder"],["Okra","ingredient/okra"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Roux","ingredient/roux"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8c/Crassostrea_gigas_p1040847.jpg/500px-Crassostrea_gigas_p1040847.jpg","w":500,"h":325,"by":"David.Monniaux","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crassostrea_gigas_p1040847.jpg"}},"ingredient/baking-soda":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/baking-soda","n":"Baking soda","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Sodium bicarbonate, commonly known as baking soda or bicarbonate of soda, or saleratus, is a chemical compound with the chemical formula NaHCO3. It is a salt composed of a sodium cation (Na+) and a bicarbonate anion (HCO3−)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-baking-soda"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baking_soda"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Afrikaner cuisine","cuisine/afrikaner-cuisine",[["Hertzoggie","dish/hertzoggie"]]],["South African cuisine","cuisine/south-african-cuisine",[["Hertzoggie","dish/hertzoggie"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Apricot jam","ingredient/apricot-jam"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Desiccated coconut","ingredient/desiccated-coconut"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Margarine","ingredient/margarine"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}]},"dish/klepon":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/klepon","n":"Klepon","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Klepon, also known as Onde-onde or Buah Melaka, is a traditional Southeast Asian confection made from glutinous rice flour filled with palm sugar and coated in grated coconut. Typically green in colour due to the use of pandan or suji leaf extract, the dough balls are boiled until the centre melts, producing a burst of sweetness when eaten. The confection is widely consumed in Indonesia, Malaysia, Brunei and Singapore, where it is commonly sold in traditional markets and classified as kue or kuih, terms for local confections often prepared for ceremonial or festive occasions. In Thailand, a similar preparation is referred to as khanom tom."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-klepon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klepon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Brown sugar","ingredient/brown-sugar"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Rice cake","ingredient/rice-cake"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-f